<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palermo Stone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 25th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> Stone is the largest fragment of an ancient Egyptian royal annal carved on a basalt slab in the 5th Dynasty, around the 25th century BCE. Kept in Palermo since 1877, it lists early kings year by year with events such as festivals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood heights and expeditions, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>'s import of forty ships of cedar. Other fragments are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palermo-stone.jpg" alt="Palermo Stone" width="320" height="535" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CvZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
